    "bmi.seo.what_is_block":
      "<h2>What is a BMI Calculator?</h2><p>A <strong>BMI (Body Mass Index) Calculator</strong> is a powerful, free online health assessment tool designed to help you determine if you have a healthy body weight relative to your height. By using the internationally recognized BMI formula, our advanced calculator provides instant, accurate results, empowering you to understand your current health status and take proactive, informed steps towards better overall wellness and longevity.</p><p>Whether you are starting a new fitness journey, monitoring your weight loss progress, or simply curious about your health metrics, our BMI calculator is here to help. It eliminates the guesswork and complex math, offering a straightforward, user-friendly experience that delivers reliable insights in seconds.</p>",
    "bmi.seo.how_to_use_block":
      '<h2>How to Use Our Advanced BMI Calculator?</h2><p>Using our BMI calculator is quick, intuitive, and takes less than 30 seconds. Follow these simple steps to get your personalized health insights:</p><h3>Step 1: Select Your Weight Unit & Enter Weight</h3><p>Choose your preferred unit of measurement: kilograms (kg), pounds (lbs), or stones. Then, input your current weight accurately.</p><h3>Step 2: Select Your Height Unit & Enter Height</h3><p>Provide your height measurement. We offer flexible options to suit your convenience: centimeters (cm), feet and inches, or meters.</p><h3>Step 3: Add Your Age and Gender</h3><p>Enter your age and select your gender. While the core BMI formula is universal, this information helps us provide more contextual and personalized health insights.</p><h3>Step 4: Calculate & View Your Results</h3><p>Click the "Calculate BMI" button. Instantly, our interactive visual gauge chart will display your exact BMI score, your weight category, and your personalized healthy weight range.</p>',
    "bmi.seo.categories_block":
      "<h2>Understanding WHO BMI Categories</h2><p>The World Health Organization (WHO) has established standard BMI categories to help classify weight status and associated health risks:</p><h3>Underweight (BMI &lt; 18.5)</h3><p>If your BMI falls below 18.5, you may be underweight. This can indicate inadequate nutrition or underlying health conditions. Consult a healthcare provider to develop a safe, healthy weight gain plan.</p><h3>Normal Weight (BMI 18.5 - 24.9)</h3><p>A BMI between 18.5 and 24.9 indicates a healthy weight range for your height. Maintain this optimal weight through balanced nutrition, regular physical activity, and adequate sleep.</p><h3>Overweight (BMI 25 - 29.9)</h3><p>A BMI of 25 to 29.9 suggests you are overweight, which may increase the risk of cardiovascular issues and diabetes. Consider adopting healthier eating habits and increasing your daily physical activity.</p><h3>Obese (BMI ≥ 30)</h3><p>A BMI of 30 or higher indicates obesity, which is associated with significant health risks. Seek professional medical advice for a comprehensive, sustainable weight management and lifestyle plan.</p>",
    "bmi.seo.formula_block":
      '<h2>The BMI Formula and Calculation</h2><p>The Body Mass Index formula is straightforward and universally accepted by medical professionals:</p><div class="formula-box"><strong>BMI = weight (kg) / height² (m²)</strong></div><p>For example, if you weigh 70 kg and are 1.75 meters tall: BMI = 70 / (1.75 × 1.75) = 22.86 (Normal Weight). Our calculator automatically handles all unit conversions and complex calculations behind the scenes, ensuring 100% accurate results every single time.</p>',
    "bmi.seo.faq_block":
      "<h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) About BMI</h2><h3>What is considered a healthy BMI range?</h3><p>For most adults, a healthy BMI range is between 18.5 and 24.9. However, individual health goals may vary based on factors like age, muscle mass, bone density, and overall health conditions.</p><h3>Is BMI accurate for everyone?</h3><p>BMI is an excellent general screening tool, but it has limitations. It may not be entirely accurate for athletes or bodybuilders (who have high muscle mass), elderly individuals (who may have lost muscle), pregnant women, or growing children. Always consult a doctor for a comprehensive health assessment.</p><h3>How often should I check my BMI?</h3><p>Checking your BMI once a month is generally sufficient for tracking long-term trends. Avoid obsessive daily checking, as normal daily fluctuations in water weight can skew the results.</p>",
