    "bmi.seo.faq_block":
      "<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>What is a healthy BMI range?</h3><p>A healthy BMI range is between 18.5 and 24.9 for most adults. However, individual health goals may vary based on factors like age, muscle mass, and overall health.</p><h3>Is BMI accurate for everyone?</h3><p>BMI is a general screening tool and may not be accurate for athletes, elderly individuals, pregnant women, or those with high muscle mass.</p>",
    "bmi.seo.what_is_block":
      "<h2>What is a BMI Calculator?</h2><p>A <strong>BMI (Body Mass Index) Calculator</strong> is a powerful, free online health assessment tool designed to help you determine if you have a healthy body weight relative to your height. By using the internationally recognized BMI formula established by the World Health Organization (WHO), our advanced calculator provides instant, accurate results. This empowers you to understand your current health status and take proactive, informed steps towards better overall wellness, longevity, and disease prevention.</p><p>Whether you are starting a new fitness journey, monitoring your weight loss or muscle gain progress, or simply curious about your health metrics, our BMI calculator is here to help. It eliminates the guesswork and complex math, offering a straightforward, user-friendly experience that delivers reliable, medically sound insights in seconds.</p>",

    "bmi.seo.how_to_use_block":
      '<h2>How to Use Our Advanced BMI Calculator?</h2><p>Using our BMI calculator is quick, intuitive, and takes less than 30 seconds. Follow these simple steps to get your personalized health insights:</p><h3>Step 1: Select Your Weight Unit & Enter Weight</h3><p>Choose your preferred unit of measurement: kilograms (kg), pounds (lbs), or stones. Then, input your current weight accurately for the most precise results.</p><h3>Step 2: Select Your Height Unit & Enter Height</h3><p>Provide your height measurement. We offer flexible options to suit your convenience: centimeters (cm), feet and inches, or meters. Our system automatically handles all complex conversions behind the scenes.</p><h3>Step 3: Add Your Age and Gender</h3><p>Enter your age and select your gender. While the core BMI formula is universal, this demographic information helps us provide more contextual and personalized health insights and healthy weight ranges.</p><h3>Step 4: Calculate & View Your Results</h3><p>Click the "Calculate BMI" button. Instantly, our interactive visual gauge chart will display your exact BMI score, your weight category, and your personalized healthy weight range tailored to your specific height.</p>',

    "bmi.seo.categories_block":
      "<h2>Understanding WHO BMI Categories</h2><p>The World Health Organization (WHO) has established standard BMI categories to help classify weight status and associated health risks for adults:</p><h3>Underweight (BMI &lt; 18.5)</h3><p>If your BMI falls below 18.5, you may be underweight. This can indicate inadequate nutrition, a fast metabolism, or underlying health conditions. It is highly recommended to consult a healthcare provider or a registered dietitian to develop a safe, healthy weight gain plan.</p><h3>Normal Weight (BMI 18.5 - 24.9)</h3><p>A BMI between 18.5 and 24.9 indicates a healthy weight range for your height. This is the optimal zone for minimizing health risks. Maintain this weight through balanced nutrition, regular physical activity, adequate sleep, and stress management.</p><h3>Overweight (BMI 25 - 29.9)</h3><p>A BMI of 25 to 29.9 suggests you are overweight, which may increase the risk of cardiovascular issues, type 2 diabetes, and joint problems. Consider adopting healthier eating habits, reducing processed foods, and increasing your daily physical activity.</p><h3>Obese (BMI ≥ 30)</h3><p>A BMI of 30 or higher indicates obesity, which is associated with significant health risks including heart disease, stroke, and certain cancers. Seek professional medical advice for a comprehensive, sustainable weight management and lifestyle modification plan.</p>",

    "bmi.seo.formula_block":
      '<h2>The BMI Formula and Calculation</h2><p>The Body Mass Index formula is straightforward and universally accepted by medical professionals worldwide:</p><div class="formula-box"><strong>BMI = weight (kg) / height² (m²)</strong></div><p>For example, if you weigh 70 kg and are 1.75 meters tall: BMI = 70 / (1.75 × 1.75) = 22.86, which falls perfectly into the Normal Weight category. Our calculator automatically handles all unit conversions (lbs to kg, feet to meters) and complex calculations behind the scenes, ensuring 100% accurate results every single time without any manual effort.</p>',

    "bmi.seo.limitations_block":
      '<h2>Limitations of BMI: Who Should Be Cautious?</h2><p>While BMI is an excellent general screening tool for the general population, it has certain limitations and may not be entirely accurate for everyone:</p><ul><li><strong>Athletes and Bodybuilders:</strong> BMI does not distinguish between muscle mass and fat. Highly muscular individuals may be classified as "overweight" or "obese" despite having low body fat and excellent health.</li><li><strong>Elderly Individuals:</strong> Older adults may have lost muscle mass and gained fat, meaning a "normal" BMI might still mask underlying health issues.</li><li><strong>Pregnant and Breastfeeding Women:</strong> Weight gain is natural and necessary during this period. BMI is not an appropriate health metric for pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Children and Teens:</strong> Growing bodies require age- and gender-specific BMI percentiles, not the standard adult formula. Always use a pediatric BMI calculator for individuals under 18.</li><li><strong>Ethnic Variations:</strong> Some ethnic groups (e.g., South Asians) may face higher health risks at lower BMI thresholds, prompting some health organizations to recommend lower cutoff points for these populations.</li></ul>',

    "bmi.seo.faq_block":
      "<h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) About BMI</h2><h3>What is considered a healthy BMI range?</h3><p>For most adults, a healthy BMI range is between 18.5 and 24.9. However, individual health goals may vary based on factors like age, muscle mass, bone density, and overall health conditions. Always consult a doctor for personalized advice.</p><h3>How often should I check my BMI?</h3><p>Checking your BMI once a month is generally sufficient for tracking long-term trends. Avoid obsessive daily checking, as normal daily fluctuations in water weight, food intake, and time of day can temporarily skew the results.</p><h3>Can I improve my BMI naturally?</h3><p>Yes. If your BMI is outside the healthy range, gradual, sustainable lifestyle changes are key. Focus on a balanced diet rich in whole foods, engage in at least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week, stay hydrated, and prioritize 7-9 hours of quality sleep per night.</p>",
